  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of an optical cavity experiment in which several small particles are trapped and driven by light. Two cavity mirrors sit at the ends, supporting a standing-wave optical field along the horizontal axis. Red, tightly focused “tweezer beams” hold blue spherical particles at selected positions inside the cavity, apparently near antinodes of the standing wave. A separate drive laser is shown entering at an angle with a polarization or phase rotation symbol, indicating that it excites motion or scattering in the trapped particles.\n\nThe key idea is that the particles couple to the cavity mode and act collectively within the resonator. Their motion or induced dipoles are aligned along the cavity axis, as suggested by the small arrows on each sphere. The sinusoidal field pattern extends between the mirrors and continues as an outgoing wave on the right, where the signal is sent “to detection.” Overall, the diagram conveys a setup for cavity optomechanics or collective light–matter coupling: optical tweezers position multiple trapped particles, a drive laser stimulates the system, and the cavity-enhanced optical response is measured at the output. Some fine details of the polarization symbols are small, but the overall mechanism is clear.",
